--TEST--
`Core\Request::bodyStream` walks `tainted bytes`, so a chunk carries the same mark `body` puts on the same octets and a plain `bytes` binding refuses it — spec § 15, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`, `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The qualifier on the *element* is the thing a streaming reader could quietly
// drop: `body()` answers `tainted string` over these same octets, so a walk
// that yielded a plain `bytes` would be a launderer reachable by writing the
// loop instead of the call. No runtime assertion can show it -- a plain value
// is assignable into a `tainted` target, so a case that only bound one would
// pass over a member that had dropped the mark. The refusal is the pin, and it
// is the binding that has to fail: `bytes` narrows through no qualifier bit.
foreach (Core\Request::bodyStream() as bytes $chunk) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted bytes`
  --> case.nvs:9:40
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
